% csv is RFC 4180: a quote is doubled, and a field is quoted when
% it holds a comma, a quote, CR or LF, or has a leading or trailing space
% (rows 6 and 10: either one alone is enough).
% The type tag does not survive -- csv has no types, so row 7's symbol and
% row 2's string are spelled alike and a reader that needs them apart wants
% text.  Row 4 spans two lines, which is what the standard asks for and what
% puts such a result out of -s's reach.
t(1, "hello, world").
t(2, "plain").
t(3, "quote \"inside\"").
t(4, "nl\nhere").
t(5, "tab\there").
t(6, " padded ").
t(7, plain).
t(8, "").
t(9, -42).
t(10, "trailing ").
t(11, "cr\rhere").

t(N, V)?
